\chapter{Using the Library}

The \texttt{thepulse-markets} package is what application code uses.
It re-exports the REST client and adds prices, local signature checks,
and OHLC history.

\section{Prices}

\lispFunctionSubsection{price}

Current index price.  The primary value is a number.  The second value
is the signed print.

With a usable key this is \texttt{get-price}.  Without one, \texttt{:btc},
\texttt{:eth}, and \texttt{:sol} come from the sample and anything else
signals \texttt{needs-key}.  \texttt{btc}, \texttt{eth}, and \texttt{sol}
are those three calls.

\texttt{:cache} defaults to nil.  A 60-second cache would hand back a
stale signed print.

\lispFunctionSubsection{prices}

One plist per symbol: \texttt{:symbol}, \texttt{:price}, \texttt{:print},
\texttt{:code}, \texttt{:message}.  One \texttt{/batch} call when a key
is set.  An unknown ticker is a row, not a failed batch.

\lispFunctionSubsection{settlement-print}

The signed print nearest a time.  Values are the price, the print, and
\texttt{deltaMs}.  \texttt{deltaMs} is the distance from the time you
asked for.  It is not signed.  The print is the nearest recorded sample,
not a price synthesized at that instant.

\lispFunctionSubsection{verity-price}

The unsigned breadth read.  \texttt{verify-print} does not apply.

\section{Verification}

The v1 canonical string is five lines:

\begin{quote}
\texttt{pulse-index-v1}\\
symbol\\
price text\\
timestamp\\
grade
\end{quote}

The price text is the \texttt{priceText} field when Pulse sent one.
Reprinting the float is a fallback and can fail a check that the
original text would pass.  If \texttt{price} and \texttt{priceText} are
different numbers, \texttt{verify-print} returns nil with reason
\texttt{:price-text-mismatch} and does not treat the print as merely
badly signed.

The signature is ECDSA P-256 with SHA-256, IEEE-P1363 \texttt{r||s},
standard base64.  The public key is the uncompressed P-256 point from
the published JWK, or the tail of the PEM.  The ring is at most 16 keys.
A \texttt{kid} selects that key.  A print with no \texttt{kid} is tried
against the ring.

\lispFunctionSubsection{verify-print}

Primary value is true or false.  The second value is a plist:
\texttt{:valid}, \texttt{:v1}, \texttt{:v2}, \texttt{:kid},
\texttt{:checked}, \texttt{:reason}.

\texttt{:version :v1} is the default.  \texttt{:v2} checks the canonical
string Pulse stored under \texttt{v2}, which includes tier, confidence,
cadence, and the interval.  Do not rebuild that string.
\texttt{:both} requires v1, and requires v2 when the block is present.
A print that has only v1 still passes \texttt{:both}.

With no \texttt{:key-ring} argument the ring is fetched, and fetched
once more if the signature fails.  A ring you pass is not refreshed.
True does not authenticate fields outside the canonical string you
checked.

\section{History}

\lispFunctionSubsection{ohlc-history}

A list of \texttt{ohlc-bar} and a meta plist.  The default window is the
last 24 hours.  \texttt{get-history} with no bounds walks from the start
of the archive; this function does not.  The default interval is one
minute.

Verified history rebuilds bars from the signed observations, so each
interval in the window is one bar even when the read spans pages.
The reconstructed dataset has candles only.  Fragments that share a
timestamp are merged: open from the first, close from the last, high the
max, low the min.  Reconstructed history is not a contemporaneous
signature.  The meta plist says which, in
\texttt{:contemporaneously-signed}.

\texttt{:limit} requests one page.  \texttt{:follow t} walks cursors,
stopping after \texttt{*history-max-pages*} (20).  \texttt{:truncated}
is true when the walk stopped early.

Volume and adjusted close are nil.  Pulse does not report trade volume.

\lispFunctionSubsection{price-history}

\texttt{(universal-time price)} pairs and the same meta plist.
Verified history returns every observation.  Reconstructed history
returns the candle close.  Universal time drops the fractional second.

\lispFunctionSubsection{ohlc-bars-for-ingest}

The tuple list \texttt{candlesticks:ingest-candlesticks} accepts:
\texttt{(time open high low close volume adjusted-close)}.  This library
does not depend on candlesticks.  The quote currency in the meta plist
is USD.

\lispTypeSubsection{ohlc-bar}

Slots \texttt{time}, \texttt{open}, \texttt{high}, \texttt{low},
\texttt{close}, \texttt{volume}, and \texttt{adjusted-close}, with
accessors \texttt{ohlc-bar-time} and so on.  \texttt{time} is a
universal time.
